{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Organizations emits CloudTrail events for account membership changes - AWS","context":"AWS Organizations now emits CloudTrail events to the management account whenever accounts join or leave the organization. These new events, AccountJoinedOrganization and AccountDepartedOrganization, provide enhanced visibility into organizational membership changes. The AccountJoinedOrganization event captures how an account joined (Created or Invited) and the join timestamp. The AccountDepartedOrganization event records how an account departed (Left for voluntary departures, Removed for account removal by the management account, or Cleaned for permanently closed accounts) and the departure timestamp. These events can be used to create CloudWatch alarms or Amazon EventBridge rules for real-time notifications, enabling rapid response to suspicious organizational changes. These CloudTrail events are available in all commercial, AWS GovCloud (US), and China Regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/aws-organizations-cloudtrail/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-cleanrooms","skill_description":"Troubleshoots and debugs AWS Clean Rooms collaboration issues related to IAM roles, S3 bucket policies, KMS keys, Lake Formation permissions, and CloudWatch logging for custom ML model training and inference jobs. Use when a customer reports permission failures, access errors, or log publishing issues in Clean Rooms.\n\nServices: cleanrooms, cleanrooms-ml, iam, s3, kms, lakeformation, cloudwatch\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, analyst\nWorkload: data-collaboration, ml\n*","skill_name":"aws-cleanrooms"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail organization trail event selector visibility rules in docs."}
